Automatic Zoom
Explainable Fake News Detection Using Traditional NLP: A 
Comparative Analysis of BoW, TF-IDF, and Machine Learning 
Classifiers 
MD Mehedul Islam, MD Fardin Rahman Mridul, Nazmul Hossain Khan, MD Rezwan Islam Shuvo and Supta 
Richard Philip 
Department of Computer Science, Faculty of Science and Technology 
American International University-Bangladesh 
Dhaka, Bangladesh 
mehedulislam7356@gmail.com, f.mridul10@gmail.com, nazkhantwo@gmail.com, rezwanislam1366@gmail.com, 
richard@aiub.edu 
Abstract—The proliferation of digital misinformation 
across online news networks poses severe challenges to 
public discourse and institutional trust. While modern deep 
neural  networks  achieve  high  classification  accuracy,  their 
opaque  internal  representations  limit  their  practical  utility 
in editorial, regulatory, and decision-support scenarios 
where  model  interpretability  is  essential.  In  this  work,  we 
present an empirical investigation into traditional, 
interpretable  machine  learning  paradigms  for  automated 
fake news detection. Using a balanced experimental corpus 
of  3,000  political  news  articles  (1,500  real  and  1,500  fake) 
sampled  from  the  Clément  Bisaillon  Kaggle  dataset,  we 
evaluate  the  comparative  impact  of  Bag-of-Words  (BoW) 
and  Term  Frequency-Inverse  Document  Frequency  (TF-
IDF) representations. We benchmark three canonical 
classifiers:  Bernoulli  Naive  Bayes,  L1-regularized  Logistic 
Regression  (Lasso),  and  linear  Support  Vector  Machines 
(SVM). Experimental evaluation on a held-out test set 
demonstrates  that  Logistic  Regression  paired  with  TF-IDF 
yields the highest predictive performance, achieving an 
accuracy of 0.9933, precision of 0.9966, recall of 0.9900, and 
an F1-score of 0.9933. Furthermore, we conduct an 
explainability  analysis  leveraging  sparse  Lasso  coefficient 
shrinkage to identify salient lexical indicators. The 
interpretability analysis reveals notable structural and 
stylistic  patterns:  authentic  articles  correlate  strongly  with 
formal journalistic attribution (e.g., 'said') and wire agency 
datelines ('reuters'), whereas fake articles frequently 
exhibit metadata tags (e.g., 'via', 'featured') and speculative 
phrasing. We discuss these findings in the context of dataset 
artifacts  and  outline  methodological  recommendations  for 
robust text classification systems. 
Index Terms—Fake news detection, Natural Language 
Processing, Bag-of-Words, TF-IDF, Logistic Regression, 
Support Vector Machines, Naive Bayes, Model 
Interpretability. 
I. INTRODUCTION 
The expansion of digital communication channels and social 
media ecosystems has accelerated information sharing while 
concurrently facilitating the dissemination of unverified and 
misleading  textual  content  [1].  Misinformation  campaigns 
and deceptive narratives can influence public opinion, 
electoral discourse, and societal trust. Developing 
automated  natural  language  processing  (NLP)  systems  that 
assist  in  distinguishing  authentic  reporting  from  fabricated 
content represents an active and practically important 
research direction [2]. 
While deep learning architectures and large language 
models (LLMs) currently establish competitive 
classification  benchmarks  in  NLP  tasks  [3],  their  complex, 
high-dimensional parameter spaces often obscure the 
specific evidence driving individual predictions [4]. In 
domains such as journalism assistance and content 
moderation, human reviewers require transparent 
justifications  for  automated  decisions.  Traditional  machine 
learning models—particularly generalized linear models and 
linear margin classifiers—provide clear mathematical 
formulations where individual feature weights directly 
reflect relative feature contributions [5]. 
In this study, we implement an explainable fake news 
classification  pipeline  using  traditional  NLP  techniques  in 
R. We analyze three central research questions: 
RQ1: How do count-based Bag-of-Words and inverse-
document-frequency-weighted TF-IDF representations 
influence the empirical performance of traditional classifiers 
on a balanced sampled news corpus? 
RQ2: Which canonical supervised classifier—among 
Bernoulli Naive Bayes, L1-regularized Logistic Regression, 
and linear Support Vector Machines—demonstrates the 
highest  predictive  consistency  across  standard  evaluation 
metrics? 
RQ3:  What  lexical  features  most  strongly  influence  the 
decision boundary of the regularized linear model, and what 
do  these  weights  reveal  regarding  linguistic  styling  and 
dataset-specific characteristics? 
The remainder of this paper is structured as follows: Section 
II reviews related literature in text classification and 
interpretability. Section III  describes the dataset, textual 
preprocessing, mathematical formulations, and experimental 
procedure.  Section  IV  presents  the  quantitative  results  and 
confusion  matrices.  Section  V  provides  the  explainability 
analysis  based  on  model  coefficients.  Section  VI  addresses 
methodological  considerations  and  limitations,  and  Section 
VII concludes the paper. 
II. RELATED WORK 
A. Text Classification for Misinformation Detection 
Computational  detection  of  deceptive  text  has  historically 
relied on linguistic and stylistic feature analysis [6]. 
Linguistic  Inquiry  and  Word  Count  (LIWC)  frameworks 
have  documented  that  deceptive  or  sensationalized  writings 
often exhibit differences in lexical complexity, pronoun 
distribution,  and  emotional  intensity  compared  to  standard 
journalistic  reporting  [6],  [7].  In  applied  machine  learning, 
early  benchmark  evaluations  demonstrated  that  supervised 
classifiers  trained  on  lexical  n-grams  and  syntactic  patterns 
provide  effective  baselines  for  identifying  misinformation 
[8], [9]. 
B. Text Representations: Bag-of-Words versus TF-IDF 
Transforming unstructured text into numerical vector spaces 
is a core prerequisite in traditional text processing pipelines 
[10]. The Bag-of-Words (BoW) model represents each 
document  through  term  occurrence  frequencies  or  binary 
presence indicators, abstracting away syntactic ordering 
while capturing lexical composition [11]. While 
computationally  straightforward,  raw  frequency  counts  can 
overemphasize ubiquitous terms [12]. 
To account for term specificity across a document 
collection,  Salton  and  Buckley  [13]  formalized  the  Term 
Frequency-Inverse Document Frequency (TF-IDF) 
weighting  approach.  In  document  classification  tasks,  TF-
IDF reduces the influence of common terms and emphasizes 
vocabulary  elements  with  higher  discriminative  specificity. 
The relative utility of BoW versus TF-IDF varies depending 
on  the  classification  architecture  and  whether  classification 
signals  are  driven  by  term  occurrence  or  term  distribution 
[14]. 
C. Linear Model Interpretability 
Interpretability  in  machine  learning  has  received  increased 
attention as automated systems are applied in societal 
contexts [15]. While post-hoc surrogate methods like LIME 
[16]  estimate  local  decision  surfaces  of  black-box  models, 
linear  models  provide  intrinsic  explainability.  In  particular, 
L1  regularization  (Lasso),  introduced  by  Tibshirani  [17], 
drives  uninformative  coefficients  to  zero,  isolating  a  sparse 
set  of  active  predictors  whose  sign  and  magnitude  directly 
indicate their contribution to class log-odds [18]. 
III. DATASET AND METHODOLOGY 
A. Dataset Description and Sampling Protocol 
The  experimental  evaluation  utilizes  data  drawn  from  the 
Fake and Real News Dataset published by Clément 
Bisaillon  on  Kaggle  [19]  (derived  from  the  University  of 
Victoria  ISOT  Research  Lab  collection  [20]).  The  source 
repository provides separate archives: authentic articles 
collected from Reuters wire services (True.csv) and 
unverified or fabricated articles sourced from flagged 
political websites and blogs (Fake.csv). 
To establish a controlled and balanced benchmark, a sample 
of  3,000  articles  was  constructed  programmatically  under  a 
fixed  random  seed  (seed  =  42).  Exactly  1,500  real  articles 
and  1,500  fake  articles  were  randomly  drawn  from  their 
respective files. The headline ('title') and article body ('text') 
were  concatenated  into  a  unified  text  attribute  ('content')  to 
provide  comprehensive  lexical  context  for  each  document. 
The combined sample was shuffled and saved. 
B. Text Preprocessing Pipeline 
The concatenated text content was processed using a 
standardized sequence of text-cleaning operations 
implemented via the R tm (Text Mining) package [21]: 
1)  URL  Removal:  Regular  expressions  (http\S+|www\S+) 
were applied to remove web addresses and hyperlinks. 
2) Alphabetic Filtering: Non-alphabetic characters and 
special  symbols  were  converted  to  whitespace  using  [^A-
Za-z ]. 
3)  Case Normalization: All text tokens were transformed to 
lowercase. 
4)  Punctuation  and  Number  Removal:  Punctuation  marks 
and numerical digits were removed using 
removePunctuation and removeNumbers. 
5)  Stopword  Filtering:  Common  English  stopwords  were 
removed using the standard stopword lexicon in tm. 
6)  Whitespace  Consolidation and Sparsity Trimming: Extra 
whitespace was stripped. A Document-Term Matrix (DTM) 
was generated across the 3,000 documents. To remove 
extremely  rare  terms  and  constrain  dimensionality,  terms 
with sparsity greater than 98% were pruned using 
removeSparseTerms(dtm, 0.98), reducing the raw 
vocabulary of 34,669 terms to 1,668 terms. 
